} :step/id #uuid "1b453cd5-7e46-54e7-bb8b-bc19b7f1fc96" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719455}}] [:step/id #uuid "217d3c47-c519-527d-a4ec-442e4aab379a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719430} :step/id #uuid "217d3c47-c519-527d-a4ec-442e4aab379a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719432}}] [:step/id #uuid "25e150a9-5cb1-5147-bb81-e8c55c6572c6" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719444 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719487} :step/id #uuid "25e150a9-5cb1-5147-bb81-e8c55c6572c6"}] [:step/id #uuid "364496c7-ecbe-5e33-98ce-7d9df4d65949" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719446 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719496} :step/id #uuid "364496c7-ecbe-5e33-98ce-7d9df4d65949" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719481}}] [:step/id #uuid "401e76bf-c294-53dc-a64d-2bfd0d9e86df" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719494} :step/id #uuid "401e76bf-c294-53dc-a64d-2bfd0d9e86df"}] [:step/id #uuid "50086270-5023-523d-8aa7-8ec755f23fe9" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719497} :step/id #uuid "50086270-5023-523d-8aa7-8ec755f23fe9"}] [:step/id #uuid "5d71f7bf-a32a-5963-b9cf-8fe1b0183278" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719431} :step/id #uuid "5d71f7bf-a32a-5963-b9cf-8fe1b0183278" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719473}}] [:step/id #uuid "624c509a-e950-59fb-a564-72ffec8b7ef6" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719477} :step/id #uuid "624c509a-e950-59fb-a564-72ffec8b7ef6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719448}}] [:step/id #uuid "6ffd5fba-aae2-507f-9cf4-fd33b14c8446" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719468} :step/id #uuid "6ffd5fba-aae2-507f-9cf4-fd33b14c8446" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719444}}] [:step/id #uuid "7d56dda3-a715-599f-9474-916f403dfde1" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719465 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719462} :step/id #uuid "7d56dda3-a715-599f-9474-916f403dfde1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719441}}] [:tutorial/id #uuid "005a3768-8410-57e5-b534-802e52d24766" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719430 :tutorial/content "**MTH 255 — Recitation Worksheet 5**\n\nDue: 2026-10-29\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline source: [Canvas — Week 5 Recitation Session](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064132/assignments/10705975), checked 2026-10-26; Canvas lists Thursday at 11:59 p.m.\nDate imported: 2026-10-26\nSource: [255-R5.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2F255-R5.pdf)\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and explicitly labeled reference sketches are independently authored additions. Source figures and problem statements are retained; domain and theorem-hypothesis qualifications are explained in the reveal fields.\n\n## Instructions\n\n- Complete all five problems and every labeled subpart. Show supporting work, including the requested sketches, proofs, and methods.\n- The PDF states that recitation worksheets are graded for completeness. Canvas describes attendance and participation grading and states that late work is not accepted.\n- Work on the printed template, write/type on the PDF with a tablet, or use your own paper with the same layout.\n- Upload one PDF to Gradescope under **Recitation Worksheet 5**. Preserve the six-page template format: a cover page followed by one page per problem, including any blank problems. If a problem uses multiple physical pages, combine them into one collage page for that problem. An incorrectly formatted PDF receives zero under the source instructions.\n- For regions with holes, use the entire positively oriented boundary: outer boundaries counterclockwise and inner boundaries clockwise. For flux, outward means outward from the shaded region, including into its holes." :tutorial/id #uuid "005a3768-8410-57e5-b534-802e52d24766" :tutorial/title "Assignment information"}] [:question/id #uuid "80878db9-2bc3-5918-ad59-784bc38c3c1e" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb1f9095-887a-5b33-be76-f21c24415e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The left edge is traversed downward from $(a,1)$ to $(a,0)$. Thus\n\n$$\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\int_1^0 f(a)\\,dy=-f(a).$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719489} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d19a61-6d40-56fa-a974-3fee4ccc5bf6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719475}] :question/id #uuid "80878db9-2bc3-5918-ad59-784bc38c3c1e" :question/problem "**(d)** Show that on the left edge of $R$, $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=-f(a)$." :question/worked-solution "Counterclockwise traversal keeps the rectangle on the left. On the left edge this requires a downward tangent, accounting for the negative sign."}] [:question/id #uuid "b7510f5f-cbaf-5458-b2ee-010c1ddc98af" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "487d69a3-5107-500f-a294-5e368fd117e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is $\\phi=xy$, and a stream function is $\\psi=(y^2-x^2)/2$. Equipotentials satisfy $xy=c$; streamlines satisfy $y^2-x^2=2k$. Draw at least two curves of each family. The families meet orthogonally away from the stagnation point at the origin.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part b](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1b.png)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719450} :answer-field/id #uuid "458f209f-05f5-5241-98e9-84a208df632c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719449}] :question/id #uuid "b7510f5f-cbaf-5458-b2ee-010c1ddc98af" :question/problem "**(b)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle y,x\\rangle$, find a potential function $\\phi$ and a stream function $\\psi$. Draw a couple of equipotential curves and streamlines.\n\n![Source vector-field diagram](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Ffield-1b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Check $\\nabla\\phi=(y,x)$, $\\psi_y=y$, and $-\\psi_x=x$. The roles of the two hyperbola families are exchanged relative to part (a), with the indicated sign change in the stream function."}] [:question/id #uuid "c4866e18-4bc1-5a67-be39-b6bc4d1e9f3a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f5f4c85-8d0a-5cbf-ad66-eb65561b455e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Under Green's usual regularity hypotheses, for example if $f\\in C^1([a,b])$, the boundary calculation from (b–d) gives\n\n$$f(b)-f(a)=\\oint_{\\partial R}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\iint_R f'(x)\\,dA=\\int_a^b\\int_0^1f'(x)\\,dy\\,dx=\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,dx.$$\n\nThis is the **Fundamental Theorem of Calculus**, in its Newton–Leibniz form.\n\nThe source assumes only that $f$ is differentiable. That alone does not ensure continuous first partial derivatives, as required by the standard version of Green's theorem used here; the formal derivation needs the additional regularity condition above (or an appropriate generalized theorem)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719467} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9da95b7-b36d-5ce9-b0d8-8239c9ee51a7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719507}] :question/id #uuid "c4866e18-4bc1-5a67-be39-b6bc4d1e9f3a" :question/problem "**(e)** Apply the circulation form of Green's theorem to $\\mathbf F$ on $R$. What theorem do you get?" :question/worked-solution "Sum the four oriented edges first: $0+f(b)+0-f(a)$. The rectangle's height is one, so the double integral reduces directly to the one-variable integral. Do not claim the standard Green argument is justified by mere differentiability without addressing this hypothesis gap."}] [:question/id #uuid "cbd43edd-2d6a-5c69-a008-f7a6c7c2cfb5" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c24e57d-8218-50a7-8f39-98149bd36b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719470} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5051d80-9e5f-57a7-93fe-c6bd449a469f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719506}] :question/id #uuid "cbd43edd-2d6a-5c69-a008-f7a6c7c2cfb5" :question/problem "**(a)** Find the area enclosed by the cardioid. **Source hint:** Use Green's theorem with $\\mathbf F=\\langle0,x\\rangle$.\n\nArea: {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=(0,x)$, the scalar curl is $1$, so Green's theorem gives $A=\\oint_C x\\,dy$. Write $\\rho(t)=1-\\sin t$, $x=\\rho\\cos t$, and $y=\\rho\\sin t$. The traversal is counterclockwise. Since $\\oint_Cd(xy)=0$,\n\n$$A=\\frac12\\oint_C(x\\,dy-y\\,dx)=\\frac12\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\rho(t)^2\\,dt.$$\n\nHere $xy'-yx'=\\rho^2$, so\n\n$$A=\\frac12\\int_0^{2\\pi}(1-2\\sin t+\\sin^2t)\\,dt=\\frac12(2\\pi+\\pi)=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}.$$\n\nThis derives the polar-area expression from the requested Green's-theorem field rather than substituting a different required method."}] [:question/id #uuid "d4d18464-6bd9-506f-8c72-09a6410dde12" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0c25130-9ad1-5acd-b4b5-3d1818d192dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f_{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719480} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cbd8f99-fe20-5c85-aa8f-36cf039177e9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719460}] :question/id #uuid "d4d18464-6bd9-506f-8c72-09a6410dde12" :question/problem "**(a)** Find $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$. Use derivative-subscript notation in the answer.\n\nScalar curl: {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=(P,Q)=(0,f(x))$, the planar scalar curl is $Q_x-P_y=f_x-0=f_x$, where $f_x=f'(x)$ for the one-variable function $f$."}] [:question/id #uuid "d804dc20-c6fb-53e1-8c66-8b204ed8bbc7" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f0924c-0e12-51a7-af01-145e16e8fdb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719456} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e09c81b-8f8b-51d0-9506-bfe9be9debce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719451}] :question/id #uuid "d804dc20-c6fb-53e1-8c66-8b204ed8bbc7" :question/problem "**(b)** Find the arc length of the cardioid.\n\n**Source hint:**\n\n$$\\sqrt{1-\\sin t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-\\sin t}\\sqrt{1+\\sin t}}{\\sqrt{1+\\sin t}}=\\frac{|\\cos t|}{\\sqrt{1+\\sin t}}.$$\n\nArc length: {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "With $\\rho=1-\\sin t$ and $\\rho'=-\\cos t$, the speed satisfies\n\n$$\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{(\\rho')^2+\\rho^2}=\\sqrt{\\cos^2t+(1-\\sin t)^2}=\\sqrt{2(1-\\sin t)}.$$\n\nUsing the supplied hint, split where $\\cos t$ changes sign: $t=\\pi/2$ and $3\\pi/2$. On the three resulting intervals, integrate $\\sqrt2|\\cos t|/\\sqrt{1+\\sin t}$ to obtain respectively $4-2\\sqrt2$, $4$, and $2\\sqrt2$. Their sum is $8$.\n\nThe quotient in the hint is undefined at $t=3\\pi/2$, even though the original speed is defined there; use the continuous limiting value in this intermediate expression. Alternatively, the identity $\\sqrt{2(1-\\sin t)}=2|\\sin(t/2-\\pi/4)|$ yields the same total length."}] [:question/id #uuid "de548bef-88d0-5a3d-b4d8-48dc735f22f5" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1885cc85-a891-5979-809e-141f9623e009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719440} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f73045-cd53-5ed1-a512-8b548add7cd5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719434}] :question/id #uuid "de548bef-88d0-5a3d-b4d8-48dc735f22f5" :question/problem "**(a)** Find the flux of $\\mathbf F=\\nabla(x^2+y^2)$ out of the shaded region.\n\n![Annulus with inner radius 1 and outer radius 2](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fannulus-3a.png)\n\nOutward flux: {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "The field is $\\mathbf F=(2x,2y)$, with divergence $2+2=4$. The annulus has area $\\pi(2^2-1^2)=3\\pi$, so the flux form of Green's theorem gives $4(3\\pi)=12\\pi$. Independently, the outer-circle flux is $4\\cdot4\\pi=16\\pi$, and the inner-circle flux is $(-2)\\cdot2\\pi=-4\\pi$, since outward from the annulus points toward the hole there. Their sum is $12\\pi$."}] [:question/id #uuid "df77f345-f20c-5f14-b98c-110b1ed20f2a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "678a02ec-f666-56ab-9f23-29e63201d943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is $\\phi=(x^2-y^2)/2$, and a stream function is $\\psi=xy$. Equipotentials satisfy $x^2-y^2=2c$; streamlines satisfy $xy=k$. Draw at least two curves of each family. Their regular intersections are orthogonal; the origin is a stagnation point.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part a](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1a.png)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719458} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd86abe8-9fe0-5f81-b70e-916d5ea051fa" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719484}] :question/id #uuid "df77f345-f20c-5f14-b98c-110b1ed20f2a" :question/problem "**(a)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$, find a potential function $\\phi$ and a stream function $\\psi$. Draw a couple of equipotential curves and streamlines.\n\n![Source vector-field diagram](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Ffield-1a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Check $\\nabla\\phi=(x,-y)$, $\\psi_y=x$, and $-\\psi_x=-y$. The potential curves are hyperbolas opening along the coordinate axes, while the streamlines are rectangular hyperbolas with the coordinate axes as their zero-level components."}] [:question/id #uuid "e5c996e0-6d97-56e4-b860-1a7299c3faf3" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8acfb86-65b6-5e64-a923-778070420c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "On the punctured plane, $\\phi=\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2)=\\ln r$ is a global potential. A stream function is locally $\\psi=\\theta=\\arg(x+iy)$ after choosing an angle branch. No single-valued global stream function exists on the full punctured plane.\n\nEquipotentials are circles $r=e^c$; streamlines are outward radial rays $\\theta=k$. Draw a couple of circles and a couple of rays, excluding the origin.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part c](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1c.png)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719503} :answer-field/id #uuid "797ab717-31ad-5200-80d3-40e0eff47f89" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719463}] :question/id #uuid "e5c996e0-6d97-56e4-b860-1a7299c3faf3" :question/problem "**(c)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, find a potential function $\\phi$ and a stream function $\\psi$. Draw a couple of equipotential curves and streamlines.\n\n![Source vector-field diagram](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Ffield-1c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field is undefined at the origin. Locally, $\\theta_x=-y/r^2$ and $\\theta_y=x/r^2$, so $\\psi_y=F_1$ and $-\\psi_x=F_2$. If a global stream function existed, its gradient would be $(-y/r^2,x/r^2)$, whose circulation around the unit circle is $2\\pi$, impossible for a gradient. Circle and ray families still describe equipotentials and streamlines geometrically."}] [:question/id #uuid "0090f78f-b0b8-5835-9b37-323e92c257c8" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d6d5af5-6fe8-5cff-8cde-744cf4bca8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719466}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719466} :answer-field/id #uuid "351d661a-c568-5d01-89d7-7d83c416279b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719445}] :question/id #uuid "0090f78f-b0b8-5835-9b37-323e92c257c8" :question/problem "**(a)** Find the counterclockwise circulation of $\\mathbf F=\\langle-2y,3x\\rangle$ around the boundary of the shaded region. Include every boundary component or segment, with the region on the left.\n\n![Annular sector between radii 1 and 2, excluding the right-hand sector between y=-x and y=x](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fannular-sector-2a.png)\n\nCirculation: {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "The pictured region is $1\\le r\\le2$, $\\pi/4\\le\\theta\\le7\\pi/4$. Its angular width is $3\\pi/2$, so its area is $\\tfrac12(2^2-1^2)(3\\pi/2)=9\\pi/4$. The scalar curl is $\\partial_x(3x)-\\partial_y(-2y)=3+2=5$. Green's theorem gives circulation $5(9\\pi/4)=45\\pi/4$. The boundary includes the outer arc, two radial segments, and the inner arc in its opposite orientation."}] [:question/id #uuid "03a7f72f-b44a-5c0f-ab85-f67c045f2da6" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b8da09c-3144-58f9-a42b-54e2421ef65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The counterclockwise orientation traverses the right edge upward from $(b,0)$ to $(b,1)$. Parametrize it by $\\mathbf r(t)=(b,t)$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Then\n\n$$\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\int_0^1(0,f(b))\\cdot(0,1)\\,dt=f(b).$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719459} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d42c6e-fe33-5b85-adc2-c6085a520af9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719453}] :question/id #uuid "03a7f72f-b44a-5c0f-ab85-f67c045f2da6" :question/problem "**(c)** Show that on the right edge of $R$, $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=f(b)$." :question/worked-solution "The right edge has length one, $x=b$ is fixed, and the tangent points upward. A downward parametrization would have the wrong sign for the specified boundary orientation."}] [:question/id #uuid "1f62ce36-36d9-5278-8736-218e35f12b54" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1125f9f-1f50-5c19-9257-ed8ca212b368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719493} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a634087-b4ac-5ca2-8e59-3cd0853c52df" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719437}] :question/id #uuid "1f62ce36-36d9-5278-8736-218e35f12b54" :question/problem "**(b)** Find the flux of $\\mathbf F=\\nabla\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ out of the shaded region.\n\n![Annulus with inner radius 1 and outer radius 2](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fannulus-3b.png)\n\nOutward flux: {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "For $r>0$, $\\mathbf F=(x/r,y/r)$ and $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=1/r$. The origin is outside the annulus, so Green's theorem applies on this region:\n\n$$\\iint_R\\frac1r\\,dA=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_1^2\\frac1r\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=2\\pi.$$\n\nDirectly, the outer flux is $4\\pi$ and the inner flux is $-2\\pi$, giving $2\\pi$. Omitting the inner boundary would give the wrong answer."}] [:question/id #uuid "396b73fa-e153-52a7-b857-cef12407f39e" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8649c722-5aa8-51db-997f-16dc40aaf4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is locally $\\phi=\\theta=\\arg(x+iy)$ on a chosen angle branch; there is no single-valued global potential on the full punctured plane. A global stream function is $\\psi=-\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2)=-\\ln r$.\n\nLocal equipotentials are radial rays $\\theta=c$, and streamlines are circles $r=e^{-k}$ traversed counterclockwise. Draw a couple of rays and a couple of circles, excluding the origin.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part d](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1d.png)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719469} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eee3749-21bf-5848-8c47-26f83fcbc9d4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719471}] :question/id #uuid "396b73fa-e153-52a7-b857-cef12407f39e" :question/problem "**(d)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, find a potential function $\\phi$ and a stream function $\\psi$. Draw a couple of equipotential curves and streamlines.\n\n![Source vector-field diagram](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Ffield-1d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Check $\\psi_y=-y/r^2=F_1$ and $-\\psi_x=x/r^2=F_2$. The field's unit-circle circulation is $2\\pi$, ruling out a global potential. A continuous angle branch works on a cut domain, for example $\\theta=\\arctan(y/x)$ on $x>0$. Additive constants in any valid potential or stream function do not change the field."}] [:question/id #uuid "6723d5bd-2fd9-5cdf-9e93-3864827f6161" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53d3a8b-59b0-5ee4-bf47-61205da79458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719479} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6a9e3a-5016-57c4-8f06-962ad56b5407" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719491}] :question/id #uuid "6723d5bd-2fd9-5cdf-9e93-3864827f6161" :question/problem "**(b)** Find the counterclockwise circulation of $\\mathbf F=\\langle0,xy\\rangle$ around the boundary of the shaded region.\n\n![Upper half-annulus between radii 1 and 2](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fupper-half-annulus-2b.png)\n\nCirculation: {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "Here $1\\le r\\le2$, $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$, and $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\partial_x(xy)=y$. Hence\n\n$$\\oint_{\\partial R}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\iint_R y\\,dA=\\int_0^\\pi\\int_1^2 r^2\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\frac73\\cdot2=\\frac{14}{3}.$$\n\nThe entire boundary is used, including the inner semicircle. The field is smooth on a neighborhood of the region."}] [:question/id #uuid "7caf2745-ec66-5f08-acba-1fbed834af52" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d469e5c0-77c8-51f1-ba34-7ee6ba184feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "On either horizontal edge, $dy=0$. Since $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=0\\,dx+f(x)\\,dy$, the integrand is zero. Thus the integral along each of the top and bottom edges is zero." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719495} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3eb20ba-2856-5c6d-bb39-77a2afde1460" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719505}] :question/id #uuid "7caf2745-ec66-5f08-acba-1fbed834af52" :question/problem "**(b)** Show that on the top and bottom edges of $R$, the line integral $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ is zero." :question/worked-solution "The field is vertical, whereas the tangent to either horizontal edge is horizontal. Their dot product vanishes regardless of which horizontal edge is traversed left-to-right."}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "8eee3749-21bf-5848-8c47-26f83fcbc9d4" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8649c722-5aa8-51db-997f-16dc40aaf4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is locally $\\phi=\\theta=\\arg(x+iy)$ on a chosen angle branch; there is no single-valued global potential on the full punctured plane. A global stream function is $\\psi=-\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2)=-\\ln r$.\n\nLocal equipotentials are radial rays $\\theta=c$, and streamlines are circles $r=e^{-k}$ traversed counterclockwise. Draw a couple of rays and a couple of circles, excluding the origin.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part d](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1d.png)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719469} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eee3749-21bf-5848-8c47-26f83fcbc9d4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719471}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "99d19a61-6d40-56fa-a974-3fee4ccc5bf6" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb1f9095-887a-5b33-be76-f21c24415e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The left edge is traversed downward from $(a,1)$ to $(a,0)$. Thus\n\n$$\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\int_1^0 f(a)\\,dy=-f(a).$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719489} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d19a61-6d40-56fa-a974-3fee4ccc5bf6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719475}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "bd86abe8-9fe0-5f81-b70e-916d5ea051fa" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "678a02ec-f666-56ab-9f23-29e63201d943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is $\\phi=(x^2-y^2)/2$, and a stream function is $\\psi=xy$. Equipotentials satisfy $x^2-y^2=2c$; streamlines satisfy $xy=k$. Draw at least two curves of each family. Their regular intersections are orthogonal; the origin is a stagnation point.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part a](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1a.png)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719458} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd86abe8-9fe0-5f81-b70e-916d5ea051fa" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719484}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "cd6a9e3a-5016-57c4-8f06-962ad56b5407" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53d3a8b-59b0-5ee4-bf47-61205da79458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719479} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6a9e3a-5016-57c4-8f06-962ad56b5407" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719491}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "f3eb20ba-2856-5c6d-bb39-77a2afde1460" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d469e5c0-77c8-51f1-ba34-7ee6ba184feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "On either horizontal edge, $dy=0$. Since $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=0\\,dx+f(x)\\,dy$, the integrand is zero. Thus the integral along each of the top and bottom edges is zero." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719495} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3eb20ba-2856-5c6d-bb39-77a2afde1460" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719505}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "f5051d80-9e5f-57a7-93fe-c6bd449a469f" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c24e57d-8218-50a7-8f39-98149bd36b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719470} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5051d80-9e5f-57a7-93fe-c6bd449a469f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719506}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "f9da95b7-b36d-5ce9-b0d8-8239c9ee51a7" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f5f4c85-8d0a-5cbf-ad66-eb65561b455e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Under Green's usual regularity hypotheses, for example if $f\\in C^1([a,b])$, the boundary calculation from (b–d) gives\n\n$$f(b)-f(a)=\\oint_{\\partial R}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\iint_R f'(x)\\,dA=\\int_a^b\\int_0^1f'(x)\\,dy\\,dx=\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,dx.$$\n\nThis is the **Fundamental Theorem of Calculus**, in its Newton–Leibniz form.\n\nThe source assumes only that $f$ is differentiable. That alone does not ensure continuous first partial derivatives, as required by the standard version of Green's theorem used here; the formal derivation needs the additional regularity condition above (or an appropriate generalized theorem)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719467} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9da95b7-b36d-5ce9-b0d8-8239c9ee51a7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719507}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "04f73045-cd53-5ed1-a512-8b548add7cd5" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1885cc85-a891-5979-809e-141f9623e009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719440} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f73045-cd53-5ed1-a512-8b548add7cd5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719434}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "0a634087-b4ac-5ca2-8e59-3cd0853c52df" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1125f9f-1f50-5c19-9257-ed8ca212b368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719493} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a634087-b4ac-5ca2-8e59-3cd0853c52df" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719437}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "351d661a-c568-5d01-89d7-7d83c416279b" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d6d5af5-6fe8-5cff-8cde-744cf4bca8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719466}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719466} :answer-field/id #uuid "351d661a-c568-5d01-89d7-7d83c416279b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719445}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "458f209f-05f5-5241-98e9-84a208df632c" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "487d69a3-5107-500f-a294-5e368fd117e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is $\\phi=xy$, and a stream function is $\\psi=(y^2-x^2)/2$. Equipotentials satisfy $xy=c$; streamlines satisfy $y^2-x^2=2k$. Draw at least two curves of each family. The families meet orthogonally away from the stagnation point at the origin.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part b](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1b.png)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719450} :answer-field/id #uuid "458f209f-05f5-5241-98e9-84a208df632c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719449}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "4e09c81b-8f8b-51d0-9506-bfe9be9debce" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f0924c-0e12-51a7-af01-145e16e8fdb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719456} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e09c81b-8f8b-51d0-9506-bfe9be9debce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719451}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "54d42c6e-fe33-5b85-adc2-c6085a520af9" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b8da09c-3144-58f9-a42b-54e2421ef65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The counterclockwise orientation traverses the right edge upward from $(b,0)$ to $(b,1)$. Parametrize it by $\\mathbf r(t)=(b,t)$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Then\n\n$$\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\int_0^1(0,f(b))\\cdot(0,1)\\,dt=f(b).$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719459} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d42c6e-fe33-5b85-adc2-c6085a520af9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719453}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "6cbd8f99-fe20-5c85-aa8f-36cf039177e9" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0c25130-9ad1-5acd-b4b5-3d1818d192dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f_{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719480} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cbd8f99-fe20-5c85-aa8f-36cf039177e9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719460}] [:answer-field/id #uuid "797ab717-31ad-5200-80d3-40e0eff47f89" {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8acfb86-65b6-5e64-a923-778070420c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "On the punctured plane, $\\phi=\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2)=\\ln r$ is a global potential. A stream function is locally $\\psi=\\theta=\\arg(x+iy)$ after choosing an angle branch. No single-valued global stream function exists on the full punctured plane.\n\nEquipotentials are circles $r=e^c$; streamlines are outward radial rays $\\theta=k$. Draw a couple of circles and a couple of rays, excluding the origin.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part c](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1c.png)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719503} :answer-field/id #uuid "797ab717-31ad-5200-80d3-40e0eff47f89" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719463}] [:answer/id #uuid "8649c722-5aa8-51db-997f-16dc40aaf4af" {:answer/id #uuid "8649c722-5aa8-51db-997f-16dc40aaf4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is locally $\\phi=\\theta=\\arg(x+iy)$ on a chosen angle branch; there is no single-valued global potential on the full punctured plane. A global stream function is $\\psi=-\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2)=-\\ln r$.\n\nLocal equipotentials are radial rays $\\theta=c$, and streamlines are circles $r=e^{-k}$ traversed counterclockwise. Draw a couple of rays and a couple of circles, excluding the origin.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part d](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1d.png)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719469}] [:answer/id #uuid "8c24e57d-8218-50a7-8f39-98149bd36b89" {:answer/id #uuid "8c24e57d-8218-50a7-8f39-98149bd36b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719470}] [:answer/id #uuid "a53d3a8b-59b0-5ee4-bf47-61205da79458" {:answer/id #uuid "a53d3a8b-59b0-5ee4-bf47-61205da79458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719479}] [:answer/id #uuid "b0c25130-9ad1-5acd-b4b5-3d1818d192dd" {:answer/id #uuid "b0c25130-9ad1-5acd-b4b5-3d1818d192dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f_{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719480}] [:answer/id #uuid "cb1f9095-887a-5b33-be76-f21c24415e49" {:answer/id #uuid "cb1f9095-887a-5b33-be76-f21c24415e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The left edge is traversed downward from $(a,1)$ to $(a,0)$. Thus\n\n$$\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\int_1^0 f(a)\\,dy=-f(a).$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719489}] [:answer/id #uuid "d1125f9f-1f50-5c19-9257-ed8ca212b368" {:answer/id #uuid "d1125f9f-1f50-5c19-9257-ed8ca212b368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719493}] [:answer/id #uuid "d469e5c0-77c8-51f1-ba34-7ee6ba184feb" {:answer/id #uuid "d469e5c0-77c8-51f1-ba34-7ee6ba184feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "On either horizontal edge, $dy=0$. Since $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=0\\,dx+f(x)\\,dy$, the integrand is zero. Thus the integral along each of the top and bottom edges is zero." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719495}] [:answer/id #uuid "e8acfb86-65b6-5e64-a923-778070420c77" {:answer/id #uuid "e8acfb86-65b6-5